<article class="about-page mx-auto">
	<h2>Rules</h2>

	<h3 class="h5">Determination is done locally</h3>
	<p>
		The calendar is determined locally, so the Gregorian dates one congregation uses can differ from a congregation in another time zone.
		Some new-moon sightings are on the cusp. Halfway around the globe, the illumination of the moon and the sun may not be visible at dawn or dusk.
	</p>

	<h3 class="h5">Dates show the day of the observance</h3>
	<p>
		Each date is the day of the observance. In Torah reckoning the date starts at sundown (erev).
		Rosh Kodesh is the exception: those dates show the erev. The aim is to show every date the same way.
	</p>

	<h3 class="h5">The new moon drives the other dates</h3>
	<p>
		Purim and Hanukkah follow rabbinic sources. Every other date is driven by the new-moon dates.
		Adding a year, or dates for another time zone, is the work required to extend the calendar.
	</p>

	<h2>Questions</h2>
	<ul>
		<li>When does the new-moon determination start, relative to the spring equinox?</li>
		<li>What is the purpose of showing the waxing, waning, and full moon?</li>
		<li>How should the setting moon be understood, including the two days when the moon cannot be seen?</li>
	</ul>

